    "summary": "High‑energy set with stellar To Lay Me Down and tight second‑set jams",
    "blurb": "The show delivers a blazing guitar solo on “To Lay Me Down,” a crisp “Bertha,” and a propulsive second set that includes a seamless China Cat Sunflower > I Know You Rider transition. While the first set has occasional flat moments, the high‑energy finish makes it a must‑listen.",
    "review": "May 4, 1981 at The Spectrum captured the Dead in top form, especially on the second half of the night. Jerry’s six‑minute “To Lay Me Down” solo crackles like “purple lightning bolt,” and his tone stays on fire through the blazing “Bertha.” The classic “China Cat Sunflower > I Know You Rider” segue is tight, though a few reviewers note Bob jumping ahead on vocals. Brent’s harmonies sit solidly under the mix, while Mickey’s drumming—often described as a “pizza‑shaped drum” standing next to Jerry—holds down the groove after an early bass‑thin opening.\n\nThe soundboard source (gd81-05-04.sbd.clugston.2250.sbeok.shnf) offers the clearest picture of this high‑energy performance despite some hiss and thin low end, making it the go‑to recording. The matrix capture reveals additional gems—“Althea,” “Black Peter,” and a memorable “Cold Rain And Snow > Little Red Rooster” sequence—while an audience FOB tape showcases the punchy front‑of‑house vibe. Overall, the show is a strong second‑set showcase with enough highlights to satisfy both archivists and casual listeners.",
